Verified compiles with rustc (edition 2021): `dedup()` handles consecutive runs on original values, then in-place trimming uses byte offsets for ASCII whitespace only (safe slicing since ASCII whitespace is single-byte and never splits a UTF-8 char). Test run: `["  a  ", "  a  ", "a", " b\t", "\r\nx ", " ", "\t", "a", "a"]` → `["a", "a", "b", "x", "", "", "a"]`, and empty vec is a no-op.

```rust
pub fn squash(v: &mut Vec<String>) {
    v.dedup();
    for s in v.iter_mut() {
        let bytes = s.as_bytes();
        let ws = |b: u8| b == b' ' || (b >= 9 && b <= 13);
        let n = bytes.len();
        let mut lo = 0;
        while lo < n && ws(bytes[lo]) { lo += 1; }
        let mut hi = n;
        while hi > lo && ws(bytes[hi - 1]) { hi -= 1; }
        *s = s[lo..hi].to_string();
    }
}
```

